//? Title: The Sieve of Atkin: Primes From Quadratic Forms
//? Subtitle: Why counting solutions of 4x²+y² finds primes, how much work Atkin and Eratosthenes do, and what the primes up to 10⁸ say about the prime-counting function, with datamunge's algorithms::atkin_primes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Sieve of Eratosthenes finds primes by crossing out multiples. The Sieve of Atkin (Atkin
and Bernstein, 2004) instead flips a flag for every solution of a quadratic form such as
\(4x^2+y^2=n\), keeps the numbers flipped an odd number of times, and removes those with a
square factor. We derive why this works for the first form from Jacobi's two-square theorem,
\(r_2(n)=4\,(d_1(n)-d_3(n))\), and check the parity rule against every squarefree number up to
20,000. We count the work of both sieves and compare it with formulas derived from lattice-point
areas and Mertens' theorem. Atkin visits about \(1.04N\) lattice points while Eratosthenes
makes about \(N(\ln\ln N-\ln2+0.26)\) crossings, so in operation counts Atkin pulls ahead,
by a factor of 2.15 at \(N=10^8\). In wall-clock time, however, the simple versions here are within 10% of each other, because Atkin's work per point (a square, a remainder and a branch) costs more than a crossing. The primes up to
\(10^8\) then serve as data: Riemann's \(R(x)\) predicts \(\pi(x)=5{,}761{,}455\) at \(10^8\) to within 97, far better
than \(x/\ln x\) or \(\mathrm{li}(x)\), and primes \(\equiv3\pmod4\) lead those \(\equiv1\pmod4\)
at all but 0.03% of \(x\le10^8\) (Chebyshev's bias), with the first lead change at 26,861.
)

\toc

> Primes from quadratic forms

The sieve rests on a classical fact about sums of two squares. For any \(n\ge1\), let
\(r_2(n)\) count the integer pairs \((a,b)\), signs and order included, with \(a^2+b^2=n\), and
let \(d_1(n)\) and \(d_3(n)\) count the divisors of \(n\) that are \(\equiv1\) and \(\equiv3\pmod4\).
*Jacobi's two-square theorem* says

$$
r_2(n)=4\,\bigl(d_1(n)-d_3(n)\bigr).
$$
\alttext(r 2 of n equals 4 times d 1 of n minus d 3 of n.)

>> The first form, derived

Take \(n\equiv1\pmod4\) that is not a perfect square. In any solution of \(a^2+b^2=n\) one of
\(a,b\) is even and the other odd, since \(n\) is odd, and neither is zero. The 4 sign choices
leave \(r_2(n)/4\) solutions in positive integers, and swapping \(a\) and \(b\) pairs each
solution with even \(b\) with one with even \(a\). So exactly half, \(r_2(n)/8\), have \(b\)
even. Writing \(b=2x\) and \(a=y\), these are the positive solutions of \(4x^2+y^2=n\):

$$
\#\{x,y\ge1 : 4x^2+y^2=n\}=\frac{d_1(n)-d_3(n)}{2}.
$$
\alttext(The number of pairs x, y at least 1 with 4 x squared plus y squared equal to n equals d 1 of n minus d 3 of n, over 2.)

Now let \(n\) be squarefree, \(n=p_1\cdots p_k\). The function \(\chi(d)=+1,-1,0\) for
\(d\equiv1,3\) or even is multiplicative, and \(d_1-d_3=\sum_{d\mid n}\chi(d)=\prod_i(1+\chi(p_i))\).
If some \(p_i\equiv3\pmod4\) the product is 0, so the count is 0. Otherwise every
\(p_i\equiv1\pmod4\) and the product is \(2^k\), so the count is \(2^{k-1}\). That is odd
exactly when \(k=1\):

$$
n\ \text{squarefree},\ n\equiv1\!\!\pmod4:\qquad \#\{4x^2+y^2=n\}\ \text{is odd}\iff n\ \text{is prime}.
$$
\alttext(For n squarefree and congruent to 1 mod 4: the number of solutions of 4 x squared plus y squared equal to n is odd if and only if n is prime.)

Atkin's sieve applies this to \(n\equiv1,5\pmod{12}\) (the classes \(\equiv1\pmod4\) not
divisible by 3). The same kind of argument, using the Eisenstein integers and the form
\(x^2+3y^2\), gives the other two rules: for squarefree \(n\equiv7\pmod{12}\), \(n\) is prime iff
\(3x^2+y^2=n\) has an odd number of positive solutions, and for squarefree \(n\equiv11\pmod{12}\),
iff \(3x^2-y^2=n\) with \(x>y\) does. The remaining classes mod 12 are even or divisible by 3,
so 2 and 3 are added by hand. A number with a square factor can pass the parity test: for
example \(25=4\cdot2^2+3^2\) is its only representation. So the sieve finishes by clearing every
multiple of \(p^2\) for the primes \(p\) it has found.

```
ATKIN(N):
    flag[5..N] = false
    for each (x, y) with 4x² + y² ≤ N:            if n mod 12 ∈ {1, 5}: flip flag[n]
    for each (x, y) with 3x² + y² ≤ N:            if n mod 12 = 7:      flip flag[n]
    for each (x, y), x > y, with 3x² − y² ≤ N:    if n mod 12 = 11:     flip flag[n]
    for r = 5 .. √N: if flag[r]: clear flag[k r²] for all k
    return 2, 3 and every n with flag[n]
```

The block checks the rule directly with `dm::algorithms::atkin_form_representations()` for
the numbers up to 100, and then for every squarefree number up to 20,000.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/atkin_sieve_lattice.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto is_prime = alg::prime_sieve(20000);
  auto squarefree = [](std::uint64_t n) {
    for (std::uint64_t d = 2; d * d <= n; ++d)
      if (n % (d * d) == 0) return false;
    return true;
  };
  const char* names[] = {"4x²+y²", "3x²+y²", "3x²−y², x>y"};

  std::print("| \\(n\\) | \\(n\\bmod12\\) | form | solutions | squarefree | prime |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- | --- |\n");
  for (std::uint64_t n = 5; n <= 100; ++n) {
    alg::AtkinForm form;
    if (!alg::atkin_form_for(n, form)) continue;
    const auto count = alg::atkin_form_representations(n, form);
    if (n > 40 && !(count % 2 == 1 && !is_prime[n]) && !(n == 65 || n == 85 || n == 97)) continue;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, n % 12, names[static_cast<int>(form)], count,
               squarefree(n) ? "yes" : "no", is_prime[n] ? "yes" : "no");
  }

  std::size_t checked = 0, agree = 0, nonsquarefree_odd = 0;
  for (std::uint64_t n = 5; n <= 20000; ++n) {
    alg::AtkinForm form;
    if (!alg::atkin_form_for(n, form)) continue;
    const bool odd = alg::atkin_form_representations(n, form) % 2 == 1;
    if (!squarefree(n)) {
      nonsquarefree_odd += odd;
      continue;
    }
    ++checked;
    agree += odd == static_cast<bool>(is_prime[n]);
  }
  std::print("\nSquarefree n ≤ 20,000 with n mod 12 in {{1, 5, 7, 11}}: {} checked, {} obey the parity rule. "
             "Non-squarefree n with an odd count (removed by the square pass): {}.\n",
             checked, agree, nonsquarefree_odd);

  // The lattice points each form visits for N = 200, coloured by whether they flip a flag.
  const std::uint64_t N = 200;
  std::vector<double> x1, y1, x2, y2, xs, ys;
  for (std::uint64_t x = 1; 4 * x * x < N; ++x)
    for (std::uint64_t y = 1; 4 * x * x + y * y <= N; ++y) {
      const auto n = 4 * x * x + y * y;
      (n % 12 == 1 || n % 12 == 5 ? x1 : xs).push_back(static_cast<double>(x));
      (n % 12 == 1 || n % 12 == 5 ? y1 : ys).push_back(static_cast<double>(y));
    }
  for (std::uint64_t x = 1; 3 * x * x < N; ++x)
    for (std::uint64_t y = 1; 3 * x * x + y * y <= N; ++y) {
      const auto n = 3 * x * x + y * y;
      if (n % 12 == 7) {
        x2.push_back(static_cast<double>(x) + 0.2);
        y2.push_back(static_cast<double>(y) + 0.2);
      }
    }
  std::vector<double> ex, ey;
  for (int k = 0; k <= 100; ++k) {
    const double t = k * std::numbers::pi / 200.0;
    ex.push_back(std::sqrt(N / 4.0) * std::cos(t));
    ey.push_back(std::sqrt(static_cast<double>(N)) * std::sin(t));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(ex, ey, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.0)
      .points(xs, ys, "4x²+y², no flip", dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, 6.0)
      .points(x1, y1, "4x²+y², flips (n mod 12 = 1, 5)", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .points(x2, y2, "3x²+y², flips (n mod 12 = 7)", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5.0)
      .legend({"4x²+y²: flips", "4x²+y²: no flip", "3x²+y²: flips"},
              {dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}})
      .x_limits(0, 10)
      .y_limits(0, 17)
      .title("Lattice points of two forms for N = 200")
      .x_label("x")
      .y_label("y")
      .size(800, 600)
      .save_svg("build/atkin_sieve_lattice.svg");
}
```
