| 85 | 1 | 4x²+y² | 2 | yes | no |
| 97 | 1 | 4x²+y² | 1 | yes | yes |

Squarefree n ≤ 20,000 with n mod 12 in {1, 5, 7, 11}: 6076 checked, 6076 obey the parity rule. Non-squarefree n with an odd count (removed by the square pass): 375.
\image(figures/atkin_sieve_lattice.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Lattice points \((x,y)\) that the sieve visits for \(N=200\). Blue: \(4x^2+y^2\le200\) and the value is \(\equiv1,5\pmod{12}\), so a flag is flipped; grey: visited but skipped; red (offset slightly): \(3x^2+y^2\equiv7\pmod{12}\). The blue curve is the ellipse \(4x^2+y^2=200\).)
\alttext(Scatter plot of lattice points under the ellipse 4 x squared plus y squared equals 200. Along each column x, blue (flipping) points sit at odd y and grey (skipped) points mostly at even y, because an even y makes 4 x squared plus y squared even. Red points for the second form sit at y values 2, 4, 8 and 10 that are not multiples of 3.)

Every squarefree number checked obeys the parity rule: 6,076 of 6,076 up to 20,000. The
table shows the three ways it can go. Primes such as 13 and 97 have exactly one representation.
\(65=5\cdot13\) and \(85=5\cdot17\), products of two primes \(\equiv1\pmod4\), have two
(\(65=4\cdot1^2+7^2=4\cdot4^2+1^2\)), matching \(2^{k-1}=2\). \(35=5\cdot7\) has none. The
exception is \(25\), which is not squarefree, has one representation and is cleared only by the
final pass; 375 numbers up to 20,000 are rescued this way.

The figure shows why only about a third of the points flip a flag (0.31 of \(N\) in the next
table). For the first form, an even \(y\) makes \(n\) even, and the remaining residues mod 12 are
partly multiples of 3, so the grey points cannot hold primes. Bernstein's version skips such
points in advance by working with residues mod 60.

> How much work?

Both sieves use \(O(N)\) memory. Their operation counts can be predicted.

**Atkin.** The number of lattice points with \(x,y\ge1\) under a curve is close to the area
under it. The quarter ellipses \(4x^2+y^2\le N\) and \(3x^2+y^2\le N\) have areas
\(\tfrac{\pi}{4}\cdot\tfrac{\sqrt N}{2}\cdot\sqrt N=\tfrac{\pi N}{8}\) and
\(\tfrac{\pi}{4}\sqrt{N/3}\sqrt N=\tfrac{\pi N}{4\sqrt3}\). The region \(3x^2-y^2\le N\), \(0<y<x\)
has area \(\tfrac N4-\tfrac{N}{\sqrt3}\int_1^{\sqrt{3/2}}\!\sqrt{u^2-1}\,du\approx0.190N\). In total

$$
\text{lattice points}\approx\Bigl(\frac{\pi}{8}+\frac{\pi}{4\sqrt3}+0.190\Bigr)N\approx1.036\,N .
$$
\alttext(Lattice points is approximately pi over 8, plus pi over 4 root 3, plus 0.190, times N, which is approximately 1.036 N.)

The final pass clears \(N/p^2\) multiples for each prime \(p\ge5\), about
\(N\sum_{p\ge5}p^{-2}=N\,(P(2)-\tfrac14-\tfrac19)\approx0.091N\), where \(P(2)=\sum_p p^{-2}=0.4522\)
is the prime zeta function.

**Eratosthenes.** Crossing out the multiples of \(p\) from \(p^2\) costs \((N-p^2)/p\) steps,
so the total is \(N\sum_{p\le\sqrt N}\tfrac1p-\sum_{p\le\sqrt N}p\). Mertens' second theorem,
\(\sum_{p\le x}1/p=\ln\ln x+M+o(1)\) with \(M=0.2615\), gives

$$
\text{crossings}\approx N\bigl(\ln\ln\sqrt N+M\bigr)=N\bigl(\ln\ln N-\ln2+M\bigr),
$$
\alttext(Crossings is approximately N times log log root N plus M, which equals N times log log N minus log 2 plus M.)

which grows, slowly, faster than \(N\). The ratio of the two counts therefore grows like
\(\ln\ln N\): the asymptotic advantage of Atkin. Bernstein's optimized version also enumerates
only the residues mod 60 that can flip a flag, cutting its work to \(O(N/\ln\ln N)\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/atkin_sieve_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  using clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
  auto ms = [](clock::time_point a, clock::time_point b) { return std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(b - a).count(); };
  const double pi = std::numbers::pi, mertens = 0.2614972128;

  std::print("| \\(N\\) | \\(\\pi(N)\\) | Atkin lattice points / \\(N\\) | toggles / \\(N\\) | square removals / \\(N\\) | "
             "Eratosthenes crossings / \\(N\\) | predicted | Atkin ms | Eratosthenes ms |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> lg, atkin_ops, erat_ops, erat_pred;
  for (int k = 3; k <= 8; ++k) {
    const auto n = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(std::pow(10.0, k));
    const auto t0 = clock::now();
    const auto a = alg::atkin_sieve_work(n);
    const auto t1 = clock::now();
    const auto e = alg::eratosthenes_sieve_work(n);
    const auto t2 = clock::now();
    const double N = static_cast<double>(n);
    const double predicted = std::log(std::log(N)) - std::log(2.0) + mertens;
    std::print("| \\(10^{}\\) | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n", k, a.primes,
               a.lattice_points / N, a.toggles / N, a.square_removals / N, e.crossings / N, predicted, ms(t0, t1),
               ms(t1, t2));
    lg.push_back(k);
    atkin_ops.push_back((a.lattice_points + a.square_removals) / N);
    erat_ops.push_back(e.crossings / N);
    erat_pred.push_back(predicted);
  }
  std::print("\nPredicted Atkin lattice points / N: {:.4f}; predicted square removals / N: {:.4f}.\n",
             pi / 8 + pi / (4 * std::sqrt(3.0)) + 0.25 - (std::sqrt(0.75) - std::log(std::sqrt(1.5) + std::sqrt(0.5))) / (2 * std::sqrt(3.0)),
             0.4522474200 - 0.25 - 1.0 / 9.0);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(lg, erat_ops, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .points(lg, erat_ops, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0)
      .lines(lg, erat_pred, "", dm::RGB{252, 165, 165}, 1.5)
      .lines(lg, atkin_ops, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(lg, atkin_ops, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .legend({"Eratosthenes", "Mertens prediction", "Atkin"},
              {dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{252, 165, 165}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}})
      .y_limits(0, 3.2)
      .title("Operations per number sieved")
      .x_label("log10 N")
      .y_label("operations / N")
      .size(900, 500)
      .save_svg("build/atkin_sieve_work.svg");
}
```
