Predicted Atkin lattice points / N: 1.0362; predicted square removals / N: 0.0911.
\image(figures/atkin_sieve_work.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Operations per number sieved: Eratosthenes' crossings (red points) with the Mertens prediction (light red), and Atkin's lattice points plus square removals (blue).)
\alttext(Line chart of operations per number against log10 N from 3 to 8. Eratosthenes rises from 1.41 to 2.43, just below the Mertens prediction curve. Atkin stays flat near 1.1.)

The counts match the derivation. Atkin's lattice points per number converge to 1.036 (the
area formula gives 1.0362; the shortfall at small \(N\) is the boundary of the region, of order
\(\sqrt N\)). The square removals approach \(P(2)-\tfrac14-\tfrac19=0.0911\). Eratosthenes'
crossings per number grow from 1.41 to 2.43 and stay 0.056 to 0.09 below
\(\ln\ln N-\ln2+M\): the sum \(\sum_{p\le\sqrt N}p\) that the formula drops is
about \(N/\ln N\), which is 0.054 of \(N\) at \(10^8\).

So at \(N=10^8\) Atkin does 1.13 operations per number against Eratosthenes' 2.43, and the gap
grows like \(\ln\ln N\). Yet the timings (about 130 ms against 140 ms on this machine) are nearly
equal. Atkin's inner loop computes a quadratic form and a remainder mod 12 and branches, while a
crossing is a single strided store. Both write to a bit array far larger than the cache, so
memory traffic dominates; practical sieves therefore segment the range, and the mod 60 wheel of
Bernstein's `primegen` is what makes Atkin competitive in practice.

> The primes up to 10⁸ as data

>> How many primes?

The prime number theorem says \(\pi(x)\sim x/\ln x\). Gauss's better guess is the logarithmic
integral \(\mathrm{li}(x)=\int_0^x dt/\ln t\), and Riemann's is
\(R(x)=\sum_{n\ge1}\tfrac{\mu(n)}{n}\mathrm{li}(x^{1/n})\), which subtracts the overcount of prime
powers that \(\mathrm{li}\) implicitly includes. `logarithmic_integral()` and
`riemann_prime_count()` evaluate both.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/atkin_sieve_pi.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto primes = alg::atkin_primes(100000000);

  std::print("| \\(x\\) | \\(\\pi(x)\\) | \\(x/\\ln x\\) | \\(\\mathrm{{li}}(x)\\) | \\(R(x)\\) | "
             "\\(\\pi-x/\\ln x\\) | \\(\\pi-\\mathrm{{li}}\\) | \\(\\pi-R\\) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> lx, e_li, e_r;
  std::size_t count = 0;
  double next = 100.0;
  for (std::uint64_t p : primes) {
    while (static_cast<double>(p) > next) {
      const double x = next, a = x / std::log(x), l = alg::logarithmic_integral(x), r = alg::riemann_prime_count(x);
      if (std::abs(std::log10(x) - std::round(std::log10(x))) < 1e-9)
        std::print("| \\(10^{:.0f}\\) | {} | {:.0f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.0f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n", std::log10(x), count, a,
                   l, r, count - a, count - l, count - r);
      lx.push_back(std::log10(x));
      e_li.push_back((count - l) / std::sqrt(x) * std::log(x));
      e_r.push_back((count - r) / std::sqrt(x) * std::log(x));
      next *= std::pow(10.0, 0.05);
    }
    ++count;
  }
  const double x = 1e8;
  std::print("| \\(10^8\\) | {} | {:.0f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.0f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |\n", count, x / std::log(x),
             alg::logarithmic_integral(x), alg::riemann_prime_count(x), count - x / std::log(x),
             count - alg::logarithmic_integral(x), count - alg::riemann_prime_count(x));

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(lx, e_li, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .lines(lx, e_r, "", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2.0)
      .abline_h(0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .legend({"pi - li", "pi - R"}, {dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}})
      .y_limits(-3, 1)
      .title("Error of li(x) and R(x), scaled by ln x / sqrt x")
      .x_label("log10 x")
      .y_label("(pi(x) - approximation) ln x / sqrt x")
      .size(900, 500)
      .save_svg("build/atkin_sieve_pi.svg");
}
```
