| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| \(10^2\) | 25 | 22 | 30.1 | 25.7 | 3 | -5.1 | -0.7 |
| \(10^3\) | 168 | 145 | 177.6 | 168.4 | 23 | -9.6 | -0.4 |
| \(10^4\) | 1229 | 1086 | 1246.1 | 1226.9 | 143 | -17.1 | 2.1 |
| \(10^5\) | 9592 | 8686 | 9629.8 | 9587.4 | 906 | -37.8 | 4.6 |
| \(10^6\) | 78498 | 72382 | 78627.5 | 78527.4 | 6116 | -129.5 | -29.4 |
| \(10^7\) | 664579 | 620421 | 664918.4 | 664667.4 | 44158 | -339.4 | -88.4 |
| \(10^8\) | 5761455 | 5428681 | 5762209.4 | 5761551.9 | 332774 | -754.4 | -96.9 |
\image(figures/atkin_sieve_pi.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The errors \(\pi(x)-\mathrm{li}(x)\) (blue) and \(\pi(x)-R(x)\) (green), divided by \(\sqrt x/\ln x\), for \(x\) from 100 to \(10^8\). The error of \(x/\ln x\) is off the scale.)
\alttext(Two noisy lines over log10 x from 2 to 8. The pi minus R line oscillates around zero between about minus 0.5 and plus 0.5. The pi minus li line stays negative, between about minus 2.6 and minus 0.8, drifting towards minus 1.)

The table confirms \(\pi(10^8)=5{,}761{,}455\). The estimate \(x/\ln x\) is low by 5.8% at
\(10^8\). \(\mathrm{li}(x)\) is high by 754, and \(R(x)\) by 97. In the scaled plot,
\(\pi-R\) fluctuates around zero with amplitude about \(\tfrac12\sqrt x/\ln x\), the size
the Riemann hypothesis predicts for the oscillating terms from the zeros of \(\zeta\).
\(\pi-\mathrm{li}\) sits below zero, tending towards \(-1\). This follows from the second
term of \(R\): \(\mathrm{li}(x)-R(x)\approx\tfrac12\mathrm{li}(\sqrt x)\approx\sqrt x/\ln x\),
which counts the prime squares \(\mathrm{li}\) implicitly includes. \(\pi(x)<\mathrm{li}(x)\)
holds for every \(x\) anyone has computed, yet Littlewood proved it fails infinitely often;
the first failure lies beyond \(10^{19}\).

>> Chebyshev's bias

Every odd prime is \(\equiv1\) or \(\equiv3\pmod4\), and by Dirichlet's theorem the two classes
are equally common in the limit. In 1853 Chebyshev noticed that the class \(3\bmod4\) is
nearly always ahead. The sieve's first rule makes the classes concrete: the primes
\(\equiv1\pmod4\) are exactly those that are sums of two squares.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/atkin_sieve_race.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto primes = alg::atkin_primes(100000000);
  long lead = 0;  // pi(x; 4, 3) - pi(x; 4, 1)
  std::uint64_t previous = 2, ahead_one = 0, first_change = 0;
  long max_lead = 0;
  std::vector<double> x, d;
  double next = 1000;
  for (std::uint64_t p : primes) {
    if (p == 2) continue;
    // lead is constant on [previous, p): count the integers in that range where class 1 is ahead.
    if (lead < 0) ahead_one += p - previous;
    previous = p;
    const long before = lead;
    lead += p % 4 == 3 ? 1 : -1;
    if (before >= 0 && lead < 0 && first_change == 0) first_change = p;
    max_lead = std::max(max_lead, lead);
    if (static_cast<double>(p) >= next) {
      x.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(p)));
      d.push_back(lead / std::sqrt(static_cast<double>(p)) * std::log(static_cast<double>(p)));
      next *= 1.01;
    }
  }
  if (lead < 0) ahead_one += 100000000 - previous;
  std::print("| statistic | value |\n| --- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| first x with more primes 1 mod 4 than 3 mod 4 | {} |\n", first_change);
  std::print("| integers x ≤ 10⁸ where 1 mod 4 leads | {} ({:.4f}%) |\n", ahead_one, 100.0 * ahead_one / 1e8);
  std::print("| largest lead of 3 mod 4 | {} |\n", max_lead);
  std::print("| lead of 3 mod 4 at 10⁸ | {} |\n", lead);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(x, d, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.2)
      .abline_h(0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .abline_h(1, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
      .title("Lead of primes 3 mod 4 over primes 1 mod 4")
      .x_label("log10 x")
      .y_label("(pi(x;4,3) - pi(x;4,1)) ln x / sqrt x")
      .size(900, 500)
      .save_svg("build/atkin_sieve_race.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| statistic | value |
| --- | ---: |
| first x with more primes 1 mod 4 than 3 mod 4 | 26861 |
| integers x ≤ 10⁸ where 1 mod 4 leads | 30624 (0.0306%) |
| largest lead of 3 mod 4 | 1012 |
| lead of 3 mod 4 at 10⁸ | 446 |
\image(figures/atkin_sieve_race.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The lead of primes \(\equiv3\pmod4\) over primes \(\equiv1\pmod4\), scaled by \(\ln x/\sqrt x\), for \(x\) from 1,000 to \(10^8\). The red line at 1 is the average that the explicit formula predicts; below the black line the class \(1\bmod4\) is ahead.)
\alttext(Noisy line over log10 x from 3 to 8, oscillating mostly between 0.5 and 2 around the red line at 1. It approaches zero near log10 x of 5.8 and dips just below zero near 7.1.)

Primes \(\equiv1\pmod4\) first pull ahead at \(x=26{,}861\), and over all
\(x\le10^8\) they lead at only 30,624 integers, 0.03% of the range; the dip near
\(1.2\times10^7\) in the plot is one of those spells. The largest lead of \(3\bmod4\) is
1,012 and the lead at \(10^8\) is 446.

The scaled lead hovers around 1, as the explicit formula predicts. The squares of odd primes are
all \(\equiv1\pmod4\), so the prime powers counted by the natural weighted sums are
lopsided towards class 1 by about \(\pi(\sqrt x)\approx 2\sqrt x/\ln x\), and the primes
alone must lean the other way by half that, \(\sqrt x/\ln x\). Rubinstein and Sarnak (1994)
showed, assuming the generalized Riemann hypothesis, that class 3 leads on a set of
logarithmic density 0.9959, so class 1 does lead infinitely often, but rarely.

> Summary

- For squarefree \(n\equiv1\pmod4\), the number of positive solutions of \(4x^2+y^2=n\)
  is \((d_1-d_3)/2\), which is odd exactly when \(n\) is prime. All 6,076 squarefree numbers
  up to 20,000 obey the parity rules; non-squarefree numbers such as 25 need the final pass.
- Atkin visits \(1.036N\) lattice points plus \(0.091N\) square removals, as the area and
  prime-zeta formulas predict, against Eratosthenes' \(N(\ln\ln N-\ln2+M)\) crossings: 2.15
  times as many at \(10^8\). The simple implementations still run in about the same time.
- \(R(x)\) is within 97 of \(\pi(10^8)=5{,}761{,}455\); \(\mathrm{li}(x)\) overshoots by about
  \(\sqrt x/\ln x\), the prime-square correction.
- Primes \(\equiv3\pmod4\) lead at all but 0.03% of \(x\le10^8\), by about \(\sqrt x/\ln x\)
  on average.
